1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,154
A founder in Campinas has a project.

2
00:00:02,154 --> 00:00:04,923
It costs one hundred thousand reais to set up.

3
00:00:04,923 --> 00:00:10,769
If she runs it properly, it succeeds with probability eighty per cent and returns one hundred and sixty thousand.

4
00:00:10,769 --> 00:00:14,769
Eighty per cent of one hundred and sixty is one hundred and twenty-eight.

5
00:00:14,769 --> 00:00:18,769
So on average the project turns one hundred into one hundred and twenty-eight.

6
00:00:18,769 --> 00:00:20,923
It creates twenty-eight thousand reais of value.

7
00:00:20,923 --> 00:00:22,154
Nobody disputes that number.

8
00:00:22,154 --> 00:00:24,308
She has ten thousand of her own.

9
00:00:24,308 --> 00:00:25,846
She needs ninety from outside.

10
00:00:25,846 --> 00:00:27,384
She goes to a bank.

11
00:00:27,384 --> 00:00:29,231
She goes to an angel investor.

12
00:00:29,231 --> 00:00:32,923
She goes to her uncle, who is a dentist and has savings.

13
00:00:32,923 --> 00:00:37,538
Every one of them looks at the numbers and agrees: it is a good project.

14
00:00:37,538 --> 00:00:39,692
And every one of them says no.

15
00:00:39,692 --> 00:00:41,231
Here is the strange part.

16
00:00:41,231 --> 00:00:48,923
There is a famous theorem — it won two Nobel prizes between its authors — which says that in a well-functioning market this cannot happen.

17
00:00:48,923 --> 00:00:51,384
A project that creates value always finds money.

18
00:00:51,384 --> 00:00:53,230
The only question is the price.

19
00:00:53,230 --> 00:00:58,153
So either the theorem is wrong, or something about this founder is not in the theorem.

20
00:00:58,153 --> 00:01:04,923
By the end of this video you will be able to compute, to the last thousand reais, why she is turned down.

21
00:01:04,923 --> 00:01:10,769
And you will find that she is short by exactly two thousand — of something that is not money.

22
00:01:10,769 --> 00:01:13,538
That missing something is what corporate finance is about.

23
00:01:18,599 --> 00:01:21,984
Let us draw the situation, because the picture is the subject.

24
00:01:21,984 --> 00:01:23,522
In the middle, a firm.

25
00:01:23,522 --> 00:01:28,137
On the left, the people who run it — the founder, the entrepreneur, the manager.

26
00:01:28,137 --> 00:01:29,676
Tirole calls them the insiders.

27
00:01:29,676 --> 00:01:33,368
On the right, the people who fund it — shareholders, banks, bondholders.

28
00:01:33,368 --> 00:01:33,984
The outsiders.

29
00:01:33,984 --> 00:01:35,830
Money flows from right to left.

30
00:01:35,830 --> 00:01:41,676
Promises flow from left to right: a share of future income, a repayment schedule, a seat on the board.

31
00:01:41,676 --> 00:01:48,753
Now, for most of the twentieth century, finance had a beautiful general theory of how claims like these are priced: the Arrow–Debreu model.

32
00:01:48,753 --> 00:01:51,830
Complete markets, no taxes, no transaction costs, everyone knows everything.

33
00:01:51,830 --> 00:01:54,599
It is a powerful machine for pricing an asset.

34
00:01:54,599 --> 00:01:59,522
But, in Tirole's words, it "said little about the firms' financial choices and about their governance".

35
00:01:59,522 --> 00:02:03,214
In that world, what the firm does is written into the contract.

36
00:02:03,214 --> 00:02:05,984
Nobody can cheat, so there is nothing to govern.

37
00:02:05,984 --> 00:02:09,368
Corporate finance, as Tirole teaches it, begins by refusing that assumption.

38
00:02:09,368 --> 00:02:13,984
The insiders know things the outsiders do not, and do things the outsiders cannot see.

39
00:02:13,984 --> 00:02:18,907
Formally, this is a principal–agent relationship: the outsiders are the principal, the insiders are the agent.

40
00:02:18,907 --> 00:02:21,676
He sorts the problem into three kinds of asymmetry.

41
00:02:21,676 --> 00:02:29,060
First, adverse selection: the insider knows something before the contract is signed — how good the technology really is, how risky the market is.

42
00:02:29,060 --> 00:02:33,676
Second, hidden knowledge after the fact: the insider knows how much income was actually earned.

43
00:02:33,676 --> 00:02:40,753
Third, moral hazard: the outsiders cannot see how carefully projects are chosen, how much risk is taken, or how much effort goes in.

44
00:02:40,753 --> 00:02:43,214
Put our founder into each of the three.

45
00:02:43,214 --> 00:02:49,368
Adverse selection: she knows, better than any investor, whether her market really exists — and every founder says it does.

46
00:02:49,368 --> 00:02:56,137
Hidden knowledge: once the project runs, she sees the accounts first, and could report a smaller success than the one that happened.

47
00:02:56,137 --> 00:03:02,291
Moral hazard: after the money arrives, nobody sees whether she spends her days on the project or on something easier.

48
00:03:02,291 --> 00:03:05,060
All three are reasons for an outsider to hesitate.

49
00:03:05,060 --> 00:03:09,368
They differ only in timing: before the contract, after the outcome, or in between.

50
00:03:09,368 --> 00:03:11,214
Hold on to the third one.

51
00:03:11,214 --> 00:03:13,983
It is the one that turns down our founder.

52
00:03:13,983 --> 00:03:17,368
We will spend the last third of this video proving it.

53
00:03:27,396 --> 00:03:29,242
This is not a new worry.

54
00:03:29,242 --> 00:03:36,627
In nineteen thirty-two, Adolf Berle and Gardiner Means wrote a book documenting what they called the separation of ownership and control in American corporations.

55
00:03:36,627 --> 00:03:37,858
Picture a large company.

56
00:03:37,858 --> 00:03:42,473
Its shares are held by tens of thousands of people, each owning a tiny sliver.

57
00:03:42,473 --> 00:03:50,165
None of them owns enough to bother watching the managers closely — the effort would be theirs, the gain would be shared with everybody else.

58
00:03:50,165 --> 00:03:51,704
Put a number on it.

59
00:03:51,704 --> 00:03:55,396
A shareholder owns one hundredth of one per cent of a firm.

60
00:03:55,396 --> 00:04:02,165
She could spend a week reading the accounts and lobbying the board, and suppose that raises the firm's value by ten million.

61
00:04:02,165 --> 00:04:04,627
Her share of the gain is one thousand.

62
00:04:04,627 --> 00:04:12,627
The week costs her more than that — and every other shareholder collects their share of the ten million whether or not they lifted a finger.

63
00:04:12,627 --> 00:04:16,011
Monitoring is a public good among shareholders, so nobody supplies it.

64
00:04:16,011 --> 00:04:20,011
So control passes to a professional manager who owns almost none of it.

65
00:04:20,011 --> 00:04:24,627
Berle and Means showed that this dispersion "creates substantial managerial discretion, which can be abused".

66
00:04:24,627 --> 00:04:32,627
Tirole puts the premise of his entire book in one sentence: corporate insiders need not act in the best interests of the providers of the funds.

67
00:04:32,627 --> 00:04:35,704
That gives us the definition of the subject's governance half.

68
00:04:35,704 --> 00:04:45,550
Tirole adopts the one of Shleifer and Vishny: corporate governance is the set of ways in which the suppliers of finance to corporations "assure themselves of getting a return on their investment".

69
00:04:45,550 --> 00:04:48,319
Read it slowly, because every word earns its place.

70
00:04:48,319 --> 00:04:50,165
Not "how the firm maximises value".

71
00:04:50,165 --> 00:04:52,011
Not "how the board is organised".

72
00:04:52,011 --> 00:04:56,934
How the people who handed over the money make sure they get some of it back.

73
00:04:56,934 --> 00:05:01,858
He calls this the narrow and orthodox view, and it is the one this course uses.

74
00:05:01,858 --> 00:05:05,858
Notice that it is written entirely from the outsiders' side of our picture.

75
00:05:05,858 --> 00:05:07,396
Governance is the outsiders' problem.

76
00:05:12,593 --> 00:05:14,747
What exactly are the outsiders afraid of?

77
00:05:14,747 --> 00:05:20,593
Tirole's section one point one point one is titled "Moral hazard comes in many guises", and he lists four.

78
00:05:20,593 --> 00:05:24,285
Let us put them in a table, one row at a time.

79
00:05:24,285 --> 00:05:25,516
Row one: insufficient effort.

80
00:05:25,516 --> 00:05:30,132
This is not about hours at the office — most executives work very long hours.

81
00:05:30,132 --> 00:05:32,285
It is about where the effort goes.

82
00:05:32,285 --> 00:05:37,208
Avoiding the unpleasant work: cutting costs, negotiating hard with unions, firing a friend who is underperforming.

83
00:05:37,208 --> 00:05:39,362
Spreading oneself across too many outside boards.

84
00:05:39,362 --> 00:05:40,593
Row two: extravagant investments.

85
00:05:40,593 --> 00:05:42,132
Pet projects and empire building.

86
00:05:42,132 --> 00:05:46,439
Tirole's example comes from Michael Jensen on the oil industry in the late nineteen-seventies.

87
00:05:46,439 --> 00:05:55,362
Oil companies kept exploring even though, at the prices of the day, buying oil on Wall Street was much cheaper than obtaining it by drilling holes in the ground.

88
00:05:55,362 --> 00:05:58,747
The cash was there, and the managers wanted to spend it.

89
00:05:58,747 --> 00:05:59,670
Row three: entrenchment.

90
00:05:59,670 --> 00:06:02,132
Choosing investments that make the manager personally indispensable.

91
00:06:02,132 --> 00:06:06,747
Being too cautious when things go well, and gambling for resurrection when they go badly.

92
00:06:06,747 --> 00:06:07,362
Resisting takeovers.

93
00:06:07,362 --> 00:06:13,516
In parts of Europe and Japan, Tirole adds, complex cross-holdings and shares with double voting rights do the same job.

94
00:06:13,516 --> 00:06:14,439
Row four: self-dealing.

95
00:06:14,439 --> 00:06:18,439
Perks — the corporate jet, the art collection, the box at the stadium.

96
00:06:18,439 --> 00:06:23,362
RJR Nabisco, in the late nineteen-eighties, ran a fleet of ten aircraft with thirty-six company pilots.

97
00:06:23,362 --> 00:06:35,978
And at the extreme, plain theft: Tirole mentions Robert Maxwell's use of his employees' pension fund, and Tyco, where in two thousand and two the chief executive and his collaborators were found to have taken more than one hundred million dollars.

98
00:06:35,978 --> 00:06:36,593
Four rows.

99
00:06:36,593 --> 00:06:37,824
Notice what they share.

100
00:06:37,824 --> 00:06:45,516
In every one, the manager takes an action the outsiders cannot write into a contract, and that action shrinks what is left for the outsiders.

101
00:06:45,516 --> 00:06:49,208
Keep that phrase: an action that cannot be written into a contract.

102
00:06:49,208 --> 00:06:51,670
It is the hinge of the whole course.

103
00:07:04,191 --> 00:07:07,575
You could read the last table as a list of scandals.

104
00:07:07,575 --> 00:07:10,037
Tirole insists on a different picture: an iceberg.

105
00:07:10,037 --> 00:07:11,883
Above the water, the visible misbehaviour.

106
00:07:11,883 --> 00:07:14,652
A few numbers from his section on dysfunctional governance.

107
00:07:14,652 --> 00:07:23,883
Average pay of the chief executives of large American firms roughly tripled in real terms between nineteen eighty and nineteen ninety-four, and roughly doubled again by two thousand and one.

108
00:07:23,883 --> 00:07:31,575
In two thousand, the average chief executive of a large American firm earned five hundred and thirty-one times the average wage of the firm's workers.

109
00:07:31,575 --> 00:07:34,037
In nineteen eighty-two, the multiple had been forty-two.

110
00:07:34,037 --> 00:07:36,806
And is the pay at least tied to performance?

111
00:07:36,806 --> 00:07:44,498
Bertrand and Mullainathan checked whether chief executives are paid for things they control, or for luck — an oil price rise, an exchange rate move.

112
00:07:44,498 --> 00:07:50,652
Their finding, in Tirole's words: chief executive pay is as sensitive to a lucky dollar as to a general dollar.

113
00:07:50,652 --> 00:07:58,960
Interestingly, the effect is weaker where someone is watching: an additional large shareholder on the board cuts pay for luck by between twenty-three and thirty-three per cent.

114
00:07:58,960 --> 00:07:59,575
Then accounting.

115
00:07:59,575 --> 00:08:05,114
WorldCom's profits from two thousand onwards turned out to have been overstated by seven point one billion dollars.

116
00:08:05,114 --> 00:08:06,344
That is the tip.

117
00:08:06,344 --> 00:08:10,344
Now the part below the water — and this is Tirole's real point.

118
00:08:10,344 --> 00:08:13,114
What we observe is the misbehaviour that got through.

119
00:08:13,114 --> 00:08:19,883
Underneath it is everything that was prevented: the covenants, the board, the auditors, the threat of a takeover, the lender who refused.

120
00:08:19,883 --> 00:08:21,729
He calls them "the averted ones".

121
00:08:21,729 --> 00:08:23,883
This matters for how you read evidence.

122
00:08:23,883 --> 00:08:31,575
A country, or a firm, with few scandals might have excellent governance — or so little outside finance that there is nothing left to steal.

123
00:08:31,575 --> 00:08:35,575
The size of the visible tip tells you surprisingly little about the iceberg.

124
00:08:35,575 --> 00:08:38,037
And it tells you something about our founder.

125
00:08:38,037 --> 00:08:40,806
Her lenders' refusal is one of the averted ones.

126
00:08:40,806 --> 00:08:42,344
It never makes the news.

127
00:08:42,344 --> 00:08:44,806
It is governance working — at a cost.

128
00:08:52,323 --> 00:08:59,707
If the danger is that managers act against the outsiders, the obvious fix is to make the managers outsiders too: give them a stake.

129
00:08:59,707 --> 00:09:02,477
Tirole lists the instruments in section one point two.

130
00:09:02,477 --> 00:09:04,938
Monetary compensation — salary, bonus, shares, stock options.

131
00:09:04,938 --> 00:09:09,246
Implicit incentives — the threat of being fired, taken over, or pushed into bankruptcy.

132
00:09:09,246 --> 00:09:11,707
Monitoring by the board and by large investors.

133
00:09:11,707 --> 00:09:13,554
And competition in the product market.

134
00:09:13,554 --> 00:09:15,707
How strong are these incentives in practice?

135
00:09:15,707 --> 00:09:19,400
Here is the most famous measurement, and we will write it out.

136
00:09:19,400 --> 00:09:25,554
Michael Jensen and Kevin Murphy looked at the chief executives of the two hundred and fifty largest American public firms.

137
00:09:25,554 --> 00:09:29,861
The median one held a quarter of one per cent of the firm's shares.

138
00:09:29,861 --> 00:09:32,323
Now take a one-thousand-dollar increase in shareholder wealth.

139
00:09:32,323 --> 00:09:40,015
Add up everything that moves the chief executive's pocket: shares, options, this year's and next year's salary, the change in the chance of being dismissed.

140
00:09:40,015 --> 00:09:43,092
The total, on average, is three dollars and twenty-five cents.

141
00:09:43,092 --> 00:09:44,323
Three twenty-five, per thousand.

142
00:09:44,323 --> 00:09:47,400
Divide: that is about a third of one per cent.

143
00:09:47,400 --> 00:09:49,554
Tirole's comment is the line to remember.

144
00:09:49,554 --> 00:09:56,938
Suppose your grocer kept zero point three cents out of every extra dollar of profit, and gave ninety-nine point seven cents to other people.

145
00:09:56,938 --> 00:10:01,554
One might imagine that the grocer would start eating the apples on the fruit stand.

146
00:10:01,554 --> 00:10:03,400
Two caveats, both from the book.

147
00:10:03,400 --> 00:10:12,323
Later studies, by Hall and Liebman, find larger numbers once stock options are counted properly — a mean of twenty-five dollars per thousand, a median of five dollars thirty.

148
00:10:12,323 --> 00:10:19,400
And a stake can be too large: a risk-averse manager with all her wealth in one firm will refuse risky but valuable projects.

149
00:10:19,400 --> 00:10:22,169
So the question is never "incentives or no incentives".

150
00:10:22,169 --> 00:10:27,400
It is how much of the pie the insider must keep for the rest to be safe.

151
00:10:27,400 --> 00:10:28,323
Hold that sentence.

152
00:10:28,323 --> 00:10:30,477
In Act Four it becomes an equation.

153
00:10:37,989 --> 00:10:41,066
If a stake is the answer, what kind of stake?

154
00:10:41,066 --> 00:10:46,604
Tirole compares the two standard forms, and the comparison is worth drawing, because the picture does the arguing.

155
00:10:46,604 --> 00:10:51,220
On the horizontal axis, the share price P on the day the stake pays out.

156
00:10:51,220 --> 00:10:53,681
On the vertical axis, what the manager receives.

157
00:10:53,681 --> 00:10:54,912
First, a straight share.

158
00:10:54,912 --> 00:10:55,835
It pays P.

159
00:10:55,835 --> 00:11:01,373
A straight line through the origin: every real of extra value moves her pocket by the same amount.

160
00:11:01,373 --> 00:11:07,220
Second, a stock option with strike price P S — the right to buy a share at P S.

161
00:11:07,220 --> 00:11:11,835
If the price ends above the strike, she exercises and gains P minus P S.

162
00:11:11,835 --> 00:11:14,604
If it ends below, she walks away with nothing.

163
00:11:14,604 --> 00:11:23,527
So the option pays the maximum of zero and P minus P S: flat at zero, then a kink at the strike, then a line rising one for one.

164
00:11:23,527 --> 00:11:25,989
Compare them at the same cost to shareholders.

165
00:11:25,989 --> 00:11:36,758
An option is cheaper than a share, so for the same budget the firm can give many more options than shares — and above the strike, each one moves just as much as a share.

166
00:11:36,758 --> 00:11:39,835
Per real spent, options give stronger incentives near the top.

167
00:11:39,835 --> 00:11:41,681
But look at the flat part.

168
00:11:41,681 --> 00:11:49,374
If the price has fallen well below the strike, the option is "under water", out of the money, and worth little unless something remarkable happens.

169
00:11:49,374 --> 00:11:54,912
A manager holding only under-water options has nothing to lose from a wild gamble and something to win.

170
00:11:54,912 --> 00:11:58,604
That is the gamble for resurrection from Row three of our table.

171
00:11:58,604 --> 00:12:00,758
And the promise behind options is fragile.

172
00:12:00,758 --> 00:12:05,989
When the price falls, boards often reprice them — lower the strike — or grant new ones.

173
00:12:05,989 --> 00:12:11,835
Tirole's example: Ascend Communications, whose stock fell from eighty dollars to twenty-three, and whose executives' strikes were cut accordingly.

174
00:12:11,835 --> 00:12:17,989
He notes that more than half of all stock options were out of the money in two thousand and two.

175
00:12:17,989 --> 00:12:24,450
So even the instrument meant to align insiders with outsiders has a shape that, in bad states, points the other way.

176
00:12:24,450 --> 00:12:28,143
The design of the insider's slice is itself part of the problem.

177
00:12:38,188 --> 00:12:40,650
Pay is only one of Tirole's four instruments.

178
00:12:40,650 --> 00:12:42,804
The other three work through people watching.

179
00:12:42,804 --> 00:12:43,727
Implicit incentives first.

180
00:12:43,727 --> 00:12:46,188
A manager who performs badly risks being replaced.

181
00:12:46,188 --> 00:12:54,804
The evidence says the risk is real: top-executive turnover rises after poor performance, and Tirole reports that the pattern is similar in the United States, Germany and Japan.

182
00:12:54,804 --> 00:12:56,342
And financial distress sharpens it.

183
00:12:56,342 --> 00:13:04,957
Stuart Gilson found that fifty-two per cent of financially distressed firms replace senior management, against nineteen per cent of firms with equally poor stock performance but no distress.

184
00:13:04,957 --> 00:13:07,727
Debt, in other words, is itself a disciplining device.

185
00:13:07,727 --> 00:13:09,573
Hold that thought for Class eight.

186
00:13:09,573 --> 00:13:14,188
Second, monitoring, which Tirole splits in two, and the split organises half of this course.

187
00:13:14,188 --> 00:13:24,342
Active monitoring is forward-looking: someone with control rights intervenes to change what the firm will do — a large shareholder on the board, a bank renegotiating a loan, a creditor in a restructuring.

188
00:13:24,342 --> 00:13:35,419
Speculative monitoring is backward-looking: someone takes a picture of what the firm has done and acts on it without controlling anything — a stock analyst, a rating agency, a short-term lender who refuses to roll over.

189
00:13:35,419 --> 00:13:38,496
It disciplines through prices and through the threat of leaving.

190
00:13:38,496 --> 00:13:40,958
And third, the watchers have their own incentives.

191
00:13:40,958 --> 00:13:44,650
Analysts may push the shares of firms their bank wants as clients.

192
00:13:44,650 --> 00:13:57,265
Auditors may be paid more for consulting than for auditing: in two thousand and one, non-audit fees made up more than half of the fees paid to accounting firms by twenty-eight of the thirty companies in the Dow Jones Industrial Average.

193
00:13:57,265 --> 00:14:03,419
Finally, competition in the product market helps — a rival's performance is a yardstick, and a weak firm risks bankruptcy.

194
00:14:03,419 --> 00:14:07,419
But in Tirole's words, it "will never substitute for a proper governance structure".

195
00:14:14,921 --> 00:14:18,921
So far the story has an American shape: dispersed shareholders, a powerful manager.

196
00:14:18,921 --> 00:14:22,921
Tirole is careful to say that this is only one of two shapes.

197
00:14:22,921 --> 00:14:25,690
On the left, the firm Berle and Means described.

198
00:14:25,690 --> 00:14:27,228
Many small shareholders, one manager.

199
00:14:27,228 --> 00:14:29,075
Frequent, he says, in Anglo-Saxon countries.

200
00:14:29,075 --> 00:14:32,767
The conflict runs between the manager and the shareholders as a group.

201
00:14:32,767 --> 00:14:42,613
On the right, the shape typical of continental Europe: a controlling shareholder — a family, a holding company, sometimes the state — with a block of votes, and minority shareholders around it.

202
00:14:42,613 --> 00:14:45,690
Now the conflict runs between the controller and the minority.

203
00:14:45,690 --> 00:14:48,152
The controller can monitor the manager perfectly well.

204
00:14:48,152 --> 00:14:50,305
The question is who monitors the controller.

205
00:14:50,305 --> 00:14:52,152
Brazil sits firmly on the right.

206
00:14:52,152 --> 00:14:59,536
Most listed Brazilian firms have a controlling shareholder, often a family, often holding control through voting shares while the public holds non-voting preferred shares.

207
00:14:59,536 --> 00:15:07,228
The protection of minority shareholders — a phrase Tirole lists among the household terms of the field — is the Brazilian version of the problem.

208
00:15:07,228 --> 00:15:09,382
Concentrated ownership is not simply a defect.

209
00:15:09,382 --> 00:15:17,690
Tirole cites a study of seven hundred and fifty French listed firms in which two-thirds had significant family ownership — and family ownership went with better performance.

210
00:15:17,690 --> 00:15:21,382
A large owner has the incentive to watch that dispersed shareholders lack.

211
00:15:21,382 --> 00:15:27,228
And Burkart and coauthors put the logic in one line: with lower investor protection, ownership concentration is called for.

212
00:15:27,228 --> 00:15:31,536
When the law protects outsiders badly, the outsiders protect themselves by not being outsiders.

213
00:15:31,536 --> 00:15:34,305
And Brazil's institutional answers map directly onto the theory.

214
00:15:34,305 --> 00:15:46,305
The Novo Mercado listing segment, created at the start of the two thousands, admits only firms with a single class of voting shares: one share, one vote, so control cannot be held with a small slice of the capital.

215
00:15:46,305 --> 00:15:49,998
And many large Brazilian firms list American depositary receipts in New York.

216
00:15:49,998 --> 00:15:51,844
Tirole gives the reason that works.

217
00:15:51,844 --> 00:15:57,382
Countries with strong investor protection limit managers' ability to squander investor money, and so have lower agency costs.

218
00:15:57,382 --> 00:16:02,921
Cross-listing in a jurisdiction with strong protection lets a firm import that protection — and it eases financing.

219
00:16:02,921 --> 00:16:04,459
Keep the word "agency cost".

220
00:16:04,459 --> 00:16:10,613
In Act Four it will have a symbol, and our founder's whole problem will be that hers is too large.

221
00:16:15,653 --> 00:16:18,422
Now the benchmark — the theorem from the opening.

222
00:16:18,422 --> 00:16:23,961
In nineteen fifty-eight, Franco Modigliani and Merton Miller published a result that Tirole calls "striking and somewhat counterintuitive".

223
00:16:23,961 --> 00:16:32,576
Under some conditions, the total value of a firm — the value of all the claims on its income together — is independent of how it is financed.

224
00:16:32,576 --> 00:16:40,268
How much debt, how much equity, what collateral, who is senior to whom, how much is paid in dividends: none of it changes total value.

225
00:16:40,268 --> 00:16:42,422
It only changes who gets which slice.

226
00:16:42,422 --> 00:16:43,961
Tirole's image is a pie.

227
00:16:43,961 --> 00:16:48,884
Here it is, cut into a thin slice of debt and a large slice of equity.

228
00:16:48,884 --> 00:16:53,191
Now cut it again: a large slice of debt, a thin slice of equity.

229
00:16:53,191 --> 00:16:53,807
Same pie.

230
00:16:53,807 --> 00:16:58,730
In his words, "the size of the pie is unaffected by the way it is carved".

231
00:16:58,730 --> 00:17:01,191
The conditions matter, so let us list them.

232
00:17:01,191 --> 00:17:01,807
Complete markets.

233
00:17:01,807 --> 00:17:02,730
No transaction costs.

234
00:17:02,730 --> 00:17:03,345
No taxes.

235
00:17:03,345 --> 00:17:04,268
No bankruptcy costs.

236
00:17:04,268 --> 00:17:12,268
And — this is the one that is easy to miss, and Tirole spells it out a page later — the firm's decisions are themselves contractible.

237
00:17:12,268 --> 00:17:16,268
Nobody can change the size of the pie by changing what they do.

238
00:17:16,268 --> 00:17:19,960
One assumption you might expect is not on the list: risk neutrality.

239
00:17:19,960 --> 00:17:31,037
Tirole illustrates the result with risk-neutral investors because the algebra is shorter, but he notes that the proof goes through with risk aversion too, by valuing each state of the world at its own state price.

240
00:17:31,037 --> 00:17:33,807
What drives the result is not attitudes to risk.

241
00:17:33,807 --> 00:17:39,653
It is that every unit of income goes to somebody, and markets let investors undo any split they dislike.

242
00:17:39,653 --> 00:17:41,499
He calls the theorem a detonator.

243
00:17:41,499 --> 00:17:44,576
It was a benchmark whose assumptions "needed to be relaxed".

244
00:17:44,576 --> 00:17:48,268
Everything you will study in this course is one of those relaxations.

245
00:17:48,268 --> 00:17:53,499
So before relaxing anything, let us prove it — twice, the way Tirole does on page seventy-eight.

246
00:18:01,051 --> 00:18:04,128
First proof: debt and equity add up to the firm.

247
00:18:04,128 --> 00:18:08,128
Let R be the firm's random income at the end of the period.

248
00:18:08,128 --> 00:18:14,590
Careful — in this beat R is total income; later it will mean something slightly different, and I will say so.

249
00:18:14,590 --> 00:18:17,359
The firm owes its debtholders a face value D.

250
00:18:17,359 --> 00:18:20,744
Investors are risk-neutral, so a claim is worth its expected payoff.

251
00:18:20,744 --> 00:18:25,974
Tirole writes expectations with round brackets, E of something, to avoid confusion with the letter for equity.

252
00:18:25,974 --> 00:18:28,128
Line one: what do the shareholders get?

253
00:18:28,128 --> 00:18:31,820
If income exceeds D, they get what is left, R minus D.

254
00:18:31,820 --> 00:18:35,513
If income falls short, limited liability protects them and they get zero.

255
00:18:35,513 --> 00:18:38,897
So equity pays the maximum of zero and R minus D.

256
00:18:38,897 --> 00:18:41,051
Line two: what do the debtholders get?

257
00:18:41,051 --> 00:18:44,128
If income exceeds D, they are paid in full, D.

258
00:18:44,128 --> 00:18:46,590
If not, they take everything there is, R.

259
00:18:46,590 --> 00:18:49,359
So debt pays the minimum of R and D.

260
00:18:49,359 --> 00:18:51,513
Line three: add them, case by case.

261
00:18:51,513 --> 00:18:53,359
Suppose R is at least D.

262
00:18:53,359 --> 00:18:57,051
Equity gets R minus D, debt gets D; the sum is R.

263
00:18:57,051 --> 00:18:59,205
Line four: suppose R is below D.

264
00:18:59,205 --> 00:19:02,282
Equity gets zero, debt gets R; the sum is R.

265
00:19:02,282 --> 00:19:10,282
Line five: so, in every state of the world, the maximum of zero and R minus D, plus the minimum of R and D, equals R.

266
00:19:10,282 --> 00:19:13,974
The face value has cancelled out of the sum, state by state.

267
00:19:13,974 --> 00:19:16,128
Line six: take expectations of both sides.

268
00:19:16,128 --> 00:19:20,744
The value of equity plus the value of debt equals the expected value of R.

269
00:19:20,744 --> 00:19:24,128
Line seven: and the right-hand side has no D in it.

270
00:19:24,128 --> 00:19:27,205
Total value is independent of the face value of debt.

271
00:19:27,205 --> 00:19:30,282
Choose any leverage you like; the pie does not change.

272
00:19:30,282 --> 00:19:38,282
Tirole draws the conclusion immediately: since the split does not matter, the firm may as well issue a single claim — a hundred per cent equity.

273
00:19:38,282 --> 00:19:41,051
An all-equity firm is as good as any other.

274
00:19:41,051 --> 00:19:42,282
Try it with numbers.

275
00:19:42,282 --> 00:19:49,051
A firm's income is zero or two hundred, each with probability one half, so the expected value of R is one hundred.

276
00:19:49,051 --> 00:19:51,513
Set the face value of debt at eighty.

277
00:19:51,513 --> 00:19:53,667
Debt pays zero or eighty: worth forty.

278
00:19:53,667 --> 00:19:56,744
Equity pays zero or one hundred and twenty: worth sixty.

279
00:19:56,744 --> 00:19:58,282
Forty plus sixty, one hundred.

280
00:19:58,282 --> 00:20:01,359
Now raise the face value to one hundred and fifty.

281
00:20:01,359 --> 00:20:04,436
Debt pays zero or one hundred and fifty: worth seventy-five.

282
00:20:04,436 --> 00:20:06,590
Equity pays zero or fifty: worth twenty-five.

283
00:20:06,590 --> 00:20:08,436
Seventy-five plus twenty-five: one hundred again.

284
00:20:08,436 --> 00:20:10,590
The slices moved; the pie did not.

285
00:20:10,590 --> 00:20:12,128
Notice what did the work.

286
00:20:12,128 --> 00:20:14,590
Not an economic argument — an accounting identity.

287
00:20:14,590 --> 00:20:17,667
Whatever income there is, all of it goes to somebody.

288
00:20:17,667 --> 00:20:25,051
That is only interesting because, in the real world, it is false: some of the income goes to nobody, because it is never produced.

289
00:20:40,116 --> 00:20:42,270
Second proof: dividends do not matter either.

290
00:20:42,270 --> 00:20:48,424
This one is longer, so we go line by line, and I will number the lines we come back to.

291
00:20:48,424 --> 00:20:52,116
Time runs in periods: t equals zero, one, two, and so on.

292
00:20:52,116 --> 00:21:05,347
In period t the firm earns revenue R sub t, invests I sub t, has n sub t shares outstanding, pays a dividend of d sub t per share, and its shares trade at price P sub t, measured just after the dividend.

293
00:21:05,347 --> 00:21:07,808
Investors discount the future by a factor beta.

294
00:21:07,808 --> 00:21:09,347
Line one, the arbitrage condition.

295
00:21:09,347 --> 00:21:12,116
Buying a share at t costs P sub t.

296
00:21:12,116 --> 00:21:15,193
Holding it pays next period's dividend plus next period's price.

297
00:21:15,193 --> 00:21:20,116
If those were not equal in discounted expectation, investors would buy or sell until they were.

298
00:21:20,116 --> 00:21:26,885
So P sub t equals beta times the expected value of d sub t plus one, plus P sub t plus one.

299
00:21:26,885 --> 00:21:28,116
Call this equation one.

300
00:21:28,116 --> 00:21:29,962
Line two, the firm's cash identity.

301
00:21:29,962 --> 00:21:31,808
Money in must equal money out.

302
00:21:31,808 --> 00:21:41,962
In: revenue R sub t, plus whatever new shares raise — the price P sub t times the change in the number of shares, n sub t minus n sub t minus one.

303
00:21:41,962 --> 00:21:48,731
Out: dividends on the shares that were outstanding, n sub t minus one times d sub t, plus investment I sub t.

304
00:21:48,731 --> 00:21:49,962
Call this equation two.

305
00:21:49,962 --> 00:21:57,962
Line three: define the value of the firm, V sub t, as the number of shares times the price, n sub t times P sub t.

306
00:21:57,962 --> 00:22:00,731
Line four: multiply equation one by n sub t.

307
00:22:00,731 --> 00:22:09,655
Then V sub t equals beta times the expectation of n sub t times d sub t plus one, plus n sub t times P sub t plus one.

308
00:22:09,655 --> 00:22:11,808
Line five: now the step that matters.

309
00:22:11,808 --> 00:22:19,501
Write equation two one period ahead, at t plus one, and solve it for total dividends, n sub t times d sub t plus one.

310
00:22:19,501 --> 00:22:26,270
That equals revenue R sub t plus one, plus new shares issued times their price, minus investment I sub t plus one.

311
00:22:26,270 --> 00:22:28,116
Line six: substitute into line four.

312
00:22:28,116 --> 00:22:40,424
The new-share term and n sub t times P sub t plus one combine: together they are exactly n sub t plus one times P sub t plus one — which is next period's value, V sub t plus one.

313
00:22:40,424 --> 00:22:49,347
Line seven: so V sub t equals beta times the expectation of R sub t plus one, minus I sub t plus one, plus V sub t plus one.

314
00:22:49,347 --> 00:22:56,116
Line eight: repeat the substitution for V sub t plus one, then V sub t plus two, and so on, by induction.

315
00:22:56,116 --> 00:23:01,039
V sub t is the expected discounted sum, over all future periods, of revenue minus investment.

316
00:23:01,039 --> 00:23:05,039
Look at what survived: revenue and investment, the real side of the firm.

317
00:23:05,039 --> 00:23:08,424
Look at what disappeared: the dividend, and the number of shares.

318
00:23:08,424 --> 00:23:16,424
Pay out more today and you must issue shares to fund the same investment; the new shareholders' claim exactly offsets the cash the old ones received.

319
00:23:16,424 --> 00:23:20,732
Value depends only on investment policy and net income — Tirole's words, "real characteristics".

320
00:23:20,732 --> 00:23:25,347
Which, again, assumes that the investment policy does not change when the payout policy does.

321
00:23:40,383 --> 00:23:42,844
Two proofs, one message: financial structure is irrelevant.

322
00:23:42,844 --> 00:23:46,844
Now let us do the obvious thing and apply it to our founder.

323
00:23:46,844 --> 00:23:48,383
The project needs one hundred.

324
00:23:48,383 --> 00:23:50,229
Expected return, one hundred and twenty-eight.

325
00:23:50,229 --> 00:23:51,767
Net present value, plus twenty-eight.

326
00:23:51,767 --> 00:24:01,306
In the Modigliani–Miller world, the financing question has one answer: offer investors any claim worth ninety in expectation — debt, equity, it does not matter which — and keep the rest.

327
00:24:01,306 --> 00:24:03,152
Investors break even, she keeps twenty-eight.

328
00:24:03,152 --> 00:24:03,459
Funded.

329
00:24:03,459 --> 00:24:04,998
But she was not funded.

330
00:24:04,998 --> 00:24:08,383
Let us write the conclusion in red and cross it out.

331
00:24:08,383 --> 00:24:12,690
This is our wrong first attempt, and it is wrong in an instructive way.

332
00:24:12,690 --> 00:24:14,536
Nothing in either proof was mistaken.

333
00:24:14,536 --> 00:24:17,613
The mistake is in a premise we used without noticing.

334
00:24:17,613 --> 00:24:22,229
Tirole names it in one sentence, and it is the key sentence of the chapter.

335
00:24:22,229 --> 00:24:33,305
Whenever managerial decisions cannot be perfectly specified contractually, the incentives given to those who pick those decisions affect the firm's income — the size of the pie — and therefore the split of the pie matters.

336
00:24:33,305 --> 00:24:35,152
Look back at the value-additivity proof.

337
00:24:35,152 --> 00:24:40,382
It treated R as given — something that happens to the firm, the same whatever the split.

338
00:24:40,382 --> 00:24:52,075
But if the founder's effort decides whether R is one hundred and sixty or zero, and her effort depends on how much of R she keeps, then the size of the pie depends on how it is carved.

339
00:24:52,075 --> 00:24:57,305
Tirole lists what outsiders can do about it, and each item is a chapter of this course.

340
00:24:57,305 --> 00:25:02,536
They can write covenants — but covenants can only be based on public, and therefore coarse, information.

341
00:25:02,536 --> 00:25:10,536
They can give the insider high-powered incentives — a large slice — but that is costly, because the slice is no longer available to pay investors.

342
00:25:10,536 --> 00:25:20,998
Or they can delegate monitoring to a large claimholder, a bank or a big shareholder — but monitoring is partly a public good, so it is undersupplied unless someone holds a large enough stake.

343
00:25:20,998 --> 00:25:25,613
Covenants, incentives, monitoring: none is free, so the split of the pie always costs something.

344
00:25:25,613 --> 00:25:28,690
That is the whole difference between Modigliani–Miller and corporate finance.

345
00:25:28,690 --> 00:25:32,998
Before we compute it, let us see whether the data agree that financing matters.

346
00:25:32,998 --> 00:25:36,998
If the theorem were right, firms would finance themselves in no particular way.

347
00:25:36,998 --> 00:25:37,921
They do not.

348
00:25:47,982 --> 00:25:49,212
Act Three: the facts.

349
00:25:49,212 --> 00:25:55,366
If financing were irrelevant, there would be no reason for firms in every country to finance themselves the same way.

350
00:25:55,366 --> 00:25:57,520
Here is how they actually do it.

351
00:25:57,520 --> 00:26:00,905
This is Table two point two in Tirole, from Colin Mayer.

352
00:26:00,905 --> 00:26:06,443
The average financing of non-financial firms, as a percentage of all sources, from nineteen seventy to nineteen eighty-five.

353
00:26:06,443 --> 00:26:08,597
We take five countries and four rows.

354
00:26:08,597 --> 00:26:12,289
Row one: retentions — profits the firm kept instead of paying out.

355
00:26:12,289 --> 00:26:13,828
United States, sixty-seven per cent.

356
00:26:13,828 --> 00:26:14,751
United Kingdom, seventy-two.

357
00:26:14,751 --> 00:26:15,366
Germany, fifty-five.

358
00:26:15,366 --> 00:26:15,982
France, forty-four.

359
00:26:15,982 --> 00:26:16,597
Japan, thirty-four.

360
00:26:16,597 --> 00:26:17,520
Row two: loans.

361
00:26:17,520 --> 00:26:22,135
United States twenty-three, United Kingdom twenty-one, Germany twenty-one, France forty-one and a half, Japan forty-one.

362
00:26:22,135 --> 00:26:23,059
Row three: bonds.

363
00:26:23,059 --> 00:26:29,212
Under ten per cent everywhere — nearly ten in the United States, under one per cent in Britain and Germany.

364
00:26:29,212 --> 00:26:30,443
Row four: new shares.

365
00:26:30,443 --> 00:26:32,905
Under one per cent in the United States.

366
00:26:32,905 --> 00:26:33,828
Two in Germany.

367
00:26:33,828 --> 00:26:34,751
Five in Britain.

368
00:26:34,751 --> 00:26:36,597
Three and a half in Japan.

369
00:26:36,597 --> 00:26:39,674
The largest of the five is France, at under eleven.

370
00:26:39,674 --> 00:26:43,059
Tirole draws three conclusions, and the table makes each one visible.

371
00:26:43,059 --> 00:26:47,366
One: in all countries, internal financing — retained earnings — is the dominant source.

372
00:26:47,366 --> 00:26:53,520
Two: bank loans provide the bulk of external financing, well ahead of new equity, which is a small fraction everywhere.

373
00:26:53,520 --> 00:26:56,905
Three: bond markets play a minor role, except in North America.

374
00:26:56,905 --> 00:26:59,674
The countries differ in the middle of the table.

375
00:26:59,674 --> 00:27:05,520
In France and Japan, loans are about forty-one per cent of all financing — nearly as much as retentions.

376
00:27:05,520 --> 00:27:08,597
In Britain and the United States they are about twenty-two.

377
00:27:08,597 --> 00:27:13,212
But in every column, the top row beats the bottom row, by a wide margin.

378
00:27:13,212 --> 00:27:21,212
Notice the order: money the firm already has first, then a bank that can watch it closely, then — rarely — strangers on a stock exchange.

379
00:27:21,212 --> 00:27:26,135
It is the order of increasing distance between the insiders and the people supplying the money.

380
00:27:26,135 --> 00:27:31,982
A theory in which financing does not matter has nothing to say about why it should be that order.

381
00:27:44,515 --> 00:27:49,745
The share row is even smaller than it looks, because those are gross figures in some countries.

382
00:27:49,745 --> 00:27:53,130
Look at what the United States actually did in the mid-nineteen-eighties.

383
00:27:53,130 --> 00:27:56,822
Rajan and Zingales studied American firms from nineteen eighty-four to nineteen eighty-six.

384
00:27:56,822 --> 00:28:00,207
New share issues amounted to sixty-five per cent of external financing.

385
00:28:00,207 --> 00:28:01,130
That sounds large.

386
00:28:01,130 --> 00:28:04,822
But share buybacks and other equity reductions amounted to sixty-eight per cent.

387
00:28:04,822 --> 00:28:06,053
Sixty-five in, sixty-eight out.

388
00:28:06,053 --> 00:28:07,592
Net equity issuance was negative.

389
00:28:07,592 --> 00:28:10,361
In Tirole's words, basically all external financing was debt.

390
00:28:10,361 --> 00:28:18,976
And external financing itself was small: net external financing was fourteen per cent of the sum of operating cash flow and net external financing over those three years.

391
00:28:18,976 --> 00:28:22,053
Most investment was paid for with the firm's own cash.

392
00:28:22,053 --> 00:28:24,207
More recent data tell the same story.

393
00:28:24,207 --> 00:28:30,976
Here, as bars: the share of gross fixed-capital formation raised through new equity in nineteen ninety-nine, counting both first-time and later issues.

394
00:28:30,976 --> 00:28:32,515
United States, twelve per cent.

395
00:28:32,515 --> 00:28:33,438
United Kingdom, nine.

396
00:28:33,438 --> 00:28:34,053
France, nine.

397
00:28:34,053 --> 00:28:34,669
Japan, eight.

398
00:28:34,669 --> 00:28:35,284
Germany, six.

399
00:28:35,284 --> 00:28:38,053
Tirole adds a caution I want to pass on.

400
00:28:38,053 --> 00:28:39,899
Do not over-read the word "internal".

401
00:28:39,899 --> 00:28:46,361
Retained earnings are cash that shareholders agree to leave in the firm; new shares are cash shareholders hand to the firm.

402
00:28:46,361 --> 00:28:48,207
Either way, it is shareholders' money.

403
00:28:48,207 --> 00:28:51,284
The real puzzle is not where the money comes from.

404
00:28:51,284 --> 00:28:59,284
It is why handing over money one way — leaving profits in — is so much easier than the other way — buying newly issued shares.

405
00:28:59,284 --> 00:29:02,053
We will answer it in Classes eleven and twelve.

406
00:29:02,053 --> 00:29:05,746
For now, register it as a fact a theory has to match.

407
00:29:13,248 --> 00:29:15,094
Next fact: how much firms borrow.

408
00:29:15,094 --> 00:29:20,325
Table two point four, from White, on a very large sample of American firms in nineteen eighty-five.

409
00:29:20,325 --> 00:29:25,555
Two columns: equity as a share of debt plus equity, and the ratio of debt to equity.

410
00:29:25,555 --> 00:29:32,017
All industries: equity is thirty-two per cent of the total, so debt is about twice equity — two point one one.

411
00:29:32,017 --> 00:29:35,709
Tirole's summary: a typical debt–equity ratio in this sample lies around two.

412
00:29:35,709 --> 00:29:38,478
Manufacturing: equity forty-five per cent, debt-to-equity one point two.

413
00:29:38,478 --> 00:29:40,940
Wholesale and retail trade: two point four nine.

414
00:29:40,940 --> 00:29:42,478
Services: two point two five.

415
00:29:42,478 --> 00:29:45,863
And then commercial banks: equity eight per cent of the total.

416
00:29:45,863 --> 00:29:47,402
Debt is eleven times equity.

417
00:29:47,402 --> 00:29:49,863
Other studies Tirole cites fill in the extremes.

418
00:29:49,863 --> 00:29:59,709
Telephone, gas and electricity companies — regulated, with very stable revenue — had long-term debt of over half of debt plus market equity, against twenty-nine per cent for the average American firm.

419
00:29:59,709 --> 00:30:03,094
Some leveraged buyouts run debt at ten or twenty times equity.

420
00:30:03,094 --> 00:30:12,017
And despite all this variation across firms, the aggregate market-based ratio in the United States has been remarkably stable, at around zero point three two, for half a century.

421
00:30:12,017 --> 00:30:14,478
Who borrows a lot, and who does not?

422
00:30:14,478 --> 00:30:15,709
Tirole lists the pattern.

423
00:30:15,709 --> 00:30:21,863
Firms with safe, steady cash flows and assets that could be resold — buildings, aircraft, inventory — carry high debt.

424
00:30:21,863 --> 00:30:28,632
Risky firms, firms with little cash flow, and firms whose value is intangible — research, brands, growth options — carry low debt.

425
00:30:28,632 --> 00:30:31,402
Read that list again with our founder in mind.

426
00:30:31,402 --> 00:30:40,325
What makes debt easy is something an outsider can count on without trusting the insider: cash that arrives regardless of effort, an asset that can be seized and sold.

427
00:30:40,325 --> 00:30:44,632
What makes debt hard is value that depends on what the insider does next.

428
00:30:44,632 --> 00:30:48,325
That is the shape of the answer we are about to derive.

429
00:30:58,381 --> 00:31:02,688
The aggregate picture hides a second pattern: countries differ in who supplies the debt.

430
00:31:02,688 --> 00:31:09,150
Table two point five in Tirole compares the liabilities of non-financial firms in two thousand and two, from the national accounts.

431
00:31:09,150 --> 00:31:10,381
Six countries, three rows.

432
00:31:10,381 --> 00:31:13,150
Row one: securities other than stocks — essentially bonds.

433
00:31:13,150 --> 00:31:15,919
United States, fifteen point six per cent of liabilities.

434
00:31:15,919 --> 00:31:17,458
United Kingdom, ten point six.

435
00:31:17,458 --> 00:31:18,073
Japan, eight.

436
00:31:18,073 --> 00:31:18,688
France, seven.

437
00:31:18,688 --> 00:31:20,535
Germany and Italy, two point three.

438
00:31:20,535 --> 00:31:22,996
Row two: credit — loans, mostly from banks.

439
00:31:22,996 --> 00:31:24,535
United States, ten per cent.

440
00:31:24,535 --> 00:31:25,150
Germany, forty-three.

441
00:31:25,150 --> 00:31:26,688
Japan, thirty-nine and a half.

442
00:31:26,688 --> 00:31:27,304
Italy, thirty-two.

443
00:31:27,304 --> 00:31:28,227
United Kingdom, thirty-one.

444
00:31:28,227 --> 00:31:28,842
France, twenty-four.

445
00:31:28,842 --> 00:31:34,381
Row three: stocks, at market value, between twenty-eight per cent in Japan and fifty-three in Britain and France.

446
00:31:34,381 --> 00:31:38,381
So American firms borrow from markets; German and Japanese firms borrow from banks.

447
00:31:38,381 --> 00:31:41,150
The textbook names are market-based and bank-based financial systems.

448
00:31:41,150 --> 00:31:49,150
Brazil, with large public and private banks and a development bank, BNDES, supplying long-term credit, has historically looked more like the second column than the first.

449
00:31:49,150 --> 00:31:51,612
Why would that matter, if debt is debt?

450
00:31:51,612 --> 00:31:54,996
Because a bank and a bondholder watch the firm very differently.

451
00:31:54,996 --> 00:31:58,996
A bank can monitor actively, renegotiate, and learn about the borrower over time.

452
00:31:58,996 --> 00:32:02,073
A dispersed bondholder can only read the price and leave.

453
00:32:02,073 --> 00:32:09,458
Which of the two a firm faces changes what the insiders can get away with — the active and speculative monitoring we just met.

454
00:32:09,458 --> 00:32:10,689
That is Class thirteen.

455
00:32:18,247 --> 00:32:20,093
Last fact, and the most surprising.

456
00:32:20,093 --> 00:32:25,324
What happens to a firm's share price on the day it announces how it will raise money?

457
00:32:25,324 --> 00:32:29,324
Table two point six, from Eckbo and Masulis, summarising studies of American firms.

458
00:32:29,324 --> 00:32:36,093
The average two-day abnormal return when the announcement is made — the price move beyond what the market as a whole did.

459
00:32:36,093 --> 00:32:42,247
An industrial firm announces a new issue of common stock, underwritten: its share price falls three point one per cent.

460
00:32:42,247 --> 00:32:43,478
Convertible bonds: minus two.

461
00:32:43,478 --> 00:32:47,785
Straight bonds: minus zero point three, and that is not statistically different from zero.

462
00:32:47,785 --> 00:32:53,324
So the market reads a share issue as bad news, and a bond issue as roughly no news.

463
00:32:53,324 --> 00:32:58,862
And Tirole adds the other side: when a firm announces a bank loan agreement, its share price rises.

464
00:32:58,862 --> 00:33:01,016
Under Modigliani and Miller this is inexplicable.

465
00:33:01,016 --> 00:33:07,785
If the split does not change the pie, then announcing how you will split it cannot move the value of the pie.

466
00:33:07,785 --> 00:33:14,247
Yet the average firm loses three per cent of its market value on an announcement that the theorem says is irrelevant.

467
00:33:14,247 --> 00:33:22,247
The explanation — that issuing shares tells the market the insiders think the shares are overpriced — is asymmetric information, the first of Tirole's three asymmetries.

468
00:33:22,247 --> 00:33:24,093
It is Classes eleven and twelve.

469
00:33:24,093 --> 00:33:27,785
Today it is a fact, and a fact the benchmark cannot explain.

470
00:33:35,314 --> 00:33:42,391
One more family of facts, and Tirole calls it one of the best documented in corporate finance: financing moves with the business cycle.

471
00:33:42,391 --> 00:33:44,544
Six items, in a list that builds.

472
00:33:44,544 --> 00:33:46,083
One: bank finance is countercyclical.

473
00:33:46,083 --> 00:33:49,468
Firms that issue bonds in booms turn to banks in recessions.

474
00:33:49,468 --> 00:33:55,621
Two: firms with strong balance sheets extend more trade credit to weaker firms, and issue more commercial paper, in recessions.

475
00:33:55,621 --> 00:34:00,852
Three: small and medium-sized firms, which depend on banks, are hit harder by downturns than large ones.

476
00:34:00,852 --> 00:34:05,160
Four: equity issues are more frequent in upswings, both absolutely and relative to debt.

477
00:34:05,160 --> 00:34:12,237
Five: the negative price reaction to a share issue — the minus three per cent we just saw — is smaller in expansions.

478
00:34:12,237 --> 00:34:18,083
Six: firms issue shares after their own share price has risen, and buy them back after it has fallen.

479
00:34:18,083 --> 00:34:20,852
That last one has a name: equity market timing.

480
00:34:20,852 --> 00:34:24,852
Firms issue when shares are dear and buy back when they are cheap.

481
00:34:24,852 --> 00:34:32,852
Tirole lists the candidate explanations without choosing: investment really is more productive in booms; adverse selection is milder when times are good; bubbles; or irrational markets.

482
00:34:32,852 --> 00:34:34,698
For today, one observation is enough.

483
00:34:34,698 --> 00:34:38,391
If financing were irrelevant, firms would have no reason to time it.

484
00:34:38,391 --> 00:34:39,621
They time it constantly.

485
00:34:39,621 --> 00:34:49,468
And items two and three — the strong lending to the weak, the small hit harder — are the seeds of the macroeconomic story in the last two classes of the course.

486
00:34:59,514 --> 00:35:04,744
Let us collect the facts, and next to each, the part of this course that explains it.

487
00:35:04,744 --> 00:35:11,206
Firms rely on retained earnings and bank loans, and rarely issue shares: agency costs and borrowing capacity, Classes six to eight.

488
00:35:11,206 --> 00:35:15,514
Leverage tracks safe cash flow and resaleable assets: collateral and pledgeable income, Class seven.

489
00:35:15,514 --> 00:35:23,206
Share issues lower the share price, bank loans raise it: asymmetric information, Classes eleven and twelve; and monitoring by banks and large investors, Class thirteen.

490
00:35:23,206 --> 00:35:28,129
Some countries borrow from banks and others from markets: active versus speculative monitoring, Class thirteen again.

491
00:35:28,129 --> 00:35:35,513
Financing follows the business cycle, and the small are hit hardest: liquidity, Classes nine and ten, and the macroeconomic implications, Classes fifteen and sixteen.

492
00:35:35,513 --> 00:35:45,975
Tirole closes the chapter by naming what all of this points to: the key role of information and incentives in general, and of capital, liquidity, the value of collateral, and external monitoring more specifically.

493
00:35:45,975 --> 00:35:49,360
Every one of those words is a lecture in this course.

494
00:35:49,360 --> 00:35:52,129
And all of them start from the same place.

495
00:35:52,129 --> 00:35:55,206
So let us go back to the founder, and compute.

496
00:35:57,714 --> 00:35:58,329
Act Four.

497
00:35:58,329 --> 00:36:04,791
This is the model Tirole calls the workhorse of his book: the fixed-investment model of credit rationing, from Holmström and Tirole.

498
00:36:04,791 --> 00:36:06,021
Section three point two.

499
00:36:06,021 --> 00:36:07,252
A word on notation.

500
00:36:07,252 --> 00:36:10,021
From here on, R no longer means total revenue.

501
00:36:10,021 --> 00:36:13,098
It means the income the project produces if it succeeds.

502
00:36:13,098 --> 00:36:16,791
Here are the parameters, with our founder's numbers, in thousands of reais.

503
00:36:16,791 --> 00:36:18,637
I, the investment, is one hundred.

504
00:36:18,637 --> 00:36:22,637
A, her own assets — Tirole calls it net worth — is ten.

505
00:36:22,637 --> 00:36:24,791
She must borrow I minus A, ninety.

506
00:36:24,791 --> 00:36:28,175
If the project succeeds it yields R, one hundred and sixty.

507
00:36:28,175 --> 00:36:30,021
If it fails it yields zero.

508
00:36:30,021 --> 00:36:31,252
Now the moral hazard.

509
00:36:31,252 --> 00:36:34,329
After the money is raised, she chooses how to behave.

510
00:36:34,329 --> 00:36:41,406
If she behaves — she works hard, she picks the careful option — the project succeeds with probability p H, eighty per cent.

511
00:36:41,406 --> 00:36:47,560
If she misbehaves, it succeeds with probability p L, forty per cent; but she enjoys a private benefit B, twenty.

512
00:36:47,560 --> 00:36:53,098
B is whatever she gains from shirking: an easier life, a pet project, a friend hired as supplier.

513
00:36:53,098 --> 00:36:54,637
Investors cannot see her choice.

514
00:36:54,637 --> 00:36:58,637
Delta p, the difference p H minus p L, is zero point four.

515
00:36:58,637 --> 00:37:01,099
The timeline: first the loan agreement is signed.

516
00:37:01,099 --> 00:37:02,637
Then the investment is made.

517
00:37:02,637 --> 00:37:05,099
Then she chooses how to behave — unseen.

518
00:37:05,099 --> 00:37:08,175
Then the outcome is realised and the money is shared.

519
00:37:08,175 --> 00:37:15,868
Everyone is risk-neutral, the interest rate is zero, she has limited liability, and lenders are competitive: they accept any deal on which they break even.

520
00:37:15,868 --> 00:37:18,329
Each assumption is there to remove a distraction.

521
00:37:18,329 --> 00:37:23,252
Risk neutrality removes insurance motives, so the only reason to give her a slice is incentives.

522
00:37:23,252 --> 00:37:27,252
A zero interest rate removes discounting, so every number is in today's reais.

523
00:37:27,252 --> 00:37:37,714
Competition among lenders means that if the deal is financeable at all, she gets all the surplus — we are asking whether the pie can be baked, not how it is shared among lenders.

524
00:37:37,714 --> 00:37:39,560
Two assumptions, checked with our numbers.

525
00:37:39,560 --> 00:37:46,945
First, the project is good if she behaves: p H times R minus I is one hundred and twenty-eight minus one hundred, plus twenty-eight.

526
00:37:46,945 --> 00:37:55,868
Second, it is bad if she misbehaves, even counting her private benefit: p L times R minus I plus B is sixty-four minus one hundred plus twenty, minus sixteen.

527
00:37:55,868 --> 00:37:57,714
So investors want her to behave.

528
00:37:57,714 --> 00:38:00,176
The question is whether they can make her.

529
00:38:10,178 --> 00:38:12,024
The contract is a sharing rule.

530
00:38:12,024 --> 00:38:21,563
If the project succeeds, she gets R b — b for borrower — and the lenders get R l, l for lenders, with R b plus R l equal to R.

531
00:38:21,563 --> 00:38:26,486
If it fails, there is nothing, and limited liability means she cannot be made to pay.

532
00:38:26,486 --> 00:38:28,024
Line one: the incentive constraint.

533
00:38:28,024 --> 00:38:31,717
She behaves only if behaving pays at least as much as misbehaving.

534
00:38:31,717 --> 00:38:33,870
Behaving gives p H times R b.

535
00:38:33,870 --> 00:38:37,563
Misbehaving gives p L times R b, plus the private benefit B.

536
00:38:37,563 --> 00:38:42,793
So p H times R b must be at least p L times R b plus B.

537
00:38:42,793 --> 00:38:44,947
Line two: collect the R b terms.

538
00:38:44,947 --> 00:38:48,024
Delta p times R b must be at least B.

539
00:38:48,024 --> 00:38:49,870
Line three: divide by delta p.

540
00:38:49,870 --> 00:38:52,947
R b must be at least B over delta p.

541
00:38:52,947 --> 00:38:54,793
Twenty over zero point four: fifty.

542
00:38:54,793 --> 00:38:56,024
Stop on that line.

543
00:38:56,024 --> 00:39:00,947
To make her behave, she must keep at least fifty thousand reais of the success income.

544
00:39:00,947 --> 00:39:07,409
Not because she is greedy — because if her slice is any smaller, shirking pays her better, and everyone knows it.

545
00:39:07,409 --> 00:39:12,640
Why can lenders not simply punish her on failure instead — a fine if the project fails?

546
00:39:12,640 --> 00:39:13,870
Because of limited liability.

547
00:39:13,870 --> 00:39:18,794
Failure leaves nothing, and she cannot be made to pay from wealth she has already invested.

548
00:39:18,794 --> 00:39:23,409
The only lever is the reward on success, so the reward has to be large.

549
00:39:23,409 --> 00:39:29,563
If she could be fined, a small slice would do; it is the floor at zero that makes incentives expensive.

550
00:39:29,563 --> 00:39:36,332
Line four: so the most that can be promised to lenders if the project succeeds is R minus B over delta p.

551
00:39:36,332 --> 00:39:39,409
One hundred and sixty minus fifty: one hundred and ten.

552
00:39:39,409 --> 00:39:43,717
Line five: lenders are paid only on success, which happens with probability p H.

553
00:39:43,717 --> 00:39:48,640
So the most they can expect is p H times R minus B over delta p.

554
00:39:48,640 --> 00:39:51,101
Tirole calls this the pledgeable income, script P.

555
00:39:51,101 --> 00:39:53,870
Zero point eight times one hundred and ten: eighty-eight.

556
00:39:53,870 --> 00:39:56,640
Line six: compare the two numbers on the screen.

557
00:39:56,640 --> 00:40:00,640
The project's expected income is p H times R, one hundred and twenty-eight.

558
00:40:00,640 --> 00:40:02,178
The pledgeable income is eighty-eight.

559
00:40:02,178 --> 00:40:11,101
The difference, forty, is p H times B over delta p: the part of the expected income that must stay with her for the project to work at all.

560
00:40:11,101 --> 00:40:12,947
Tirole calls it the agency rent.

561
00:40:12,947 --> 00:40:14,794
There is the founder's missing something.

562
00:40:14,794 --> 00:40:20,332
Of one hundred and twenty-eight thousand reais of expected income, only eighty-eight can be promised to anyone else.

563
00:40:32,844 --> 00:40:34,382
Now ask the lenders' question.

564
00:40:34,382 --> 00:40:36,228
They put in I minus A.

565
00:40:36,228 --> 00:40:38,690
They expect back at most the pledgeable income.

566
00:40:38,690 --> 00:40:40,844
They lend only if it is enough.

567
00:40:40,844 --> 00:40:42,690
Line one: the lenders' participation constraint.

568
00:40:42,690 --> 00:40:46,382
Script P, the pledgeable income, must be at least I minus A.

569
00:40:46,382 --> 00:40:49,767
Tirole labels it I R l: individual rationality for the lenders.

570
00:40:49,767 --> 00:40:51,613
Line two: write script P out.

571
00:40:51,613 --> 00:40:56,536
p H times, R minus B over delta p, must be at least I minus A.

572
00:40:56,536 --> 00:41:00,536
Line three: move A to the left and everything else to the right.

573
00:41:00,536 --> 00:41:05,767
A must be at least I minus p H R plus p H B over delta p.

574
00:41:05,767 --> 00:41:07,921
Line four: rearrange into two recognisable pieces.

575
00:41:07,921 --> 00:41:16,228
A must be at least p H B over delta p — the agency rent — minus, p H R minus I — the net present value.

576
00:41:16,228 --> 00:41:18,382
Tirole calls the right-hand side A bar.

577
00:41:18,382 --> 00:41:20,536
This is his equation three point three.

578
00:41:20,536 --> 00:41:21,767
Line five: our numbers.

579
00:41:21,767 --> 00:41:23,305
The agency rent is forty.

580
00:41:23,305 --> 00:41:25,152
The net present value is twenty-eight.

581
00:41:25,152 --> 00:41:27,305
A bar is forty minus twenty-eight: twelve.

582
00:41:27,305 --> 00:41:28,844
Line six: she has ten.

583
00:41:28,844 --> 00:41:30,382
Ten is less than twelve.

584
00:41:30,382 --> 00:41:31,613
The lenders' constraint fails.

585
00:41:31,613 --> 00:41:32,844
She is turned down.

586
00:41:32,844 --> 00:41:34,382
Check it the other way.

587
00:41:34,382 --> 00:41:35,921
She needs ninety from lenders.

588
00:41:35,921 --> 00:41:38,382
The most they can be promised is eighty-eight.

589
00:41:38,382 --> 00:41:39,305
Short by two.

590
00:41:39,305 --> 00:41:44,844
And her own assets fall short of the threshold — ten against twelve — by the same two.

591
00:41:44,844 --> 00:41:46,690
Two ways to see one gap.

592
00:41:46,690 --> 00:41:48,844
Notice that A bar can be negative.

593
00:41:48,844 --> 00:41:58,075
If the project created, say, fifty of value instead of twenty-eight, A bar would be forty minus fifty, minus ten, and a founder with nothing at all would be funded.

594
00:41:58,075 --> 00:42:06,690
Tirole assumes A bar is positive precisely to study the interesting case: when the value the project creates is smaller than the rent its manager must be left.

595
00:42:06,690 --> 00:42:08,536
Read the threshold as a sentence.

596
00:42:08,536 --> 00:42:13,152
The founder must bring at least the agency rent, minus the value the project creates.

597
00:42:13,152 --> 00:42:18,690
If the project creates more value than the rent she must keep, she needs nothing of her own.

598
00:42:18,690 --> 00:42:22,075
If the rent is bigger, she must cover the difference herself.

599
00:42:22,075 --> 00:42:26,075
As Tirole puts it, quoting conventional wisdom: one only lends to the rich.

600
00:42:43,842 --> 00:42:48,458
To see that the threshold is the whole story, give the founder a richer sister.

601
00:42:48,458 --> 00:42:50,612
Same project, same behaviour, same private benefit.

602
00:42:50,612 --> 00:42:53,996
The sister has twenty thousand reais of her own, not ten.

603
00:42:53,996 --> 00:42:56,765
Twenty is above twelve, so she should be funded.

604
00:42:56,765 --> 00:43:00,150
Let us write her contract out and check that it works.

605
00:43:00,150 --> 00:43:03,842
Line one: she borrows I minus A, one hundred minus twenty: eighty.

606
00:43:03,842 --> 00:43:05,689
Line two: competitive lenders break even.

607
00:43:05,689 --> 00:43:09,073
They are paid R l on success, with probability p H.

608
00:43:09,073 --> 00:43:11,535
So p H times R l equals eighty.

609
00:43:11,535 --> 00:43:14,612
R l is eighty over zero point eight: one hundred.

610
00:43:14,612 --> 00:43:17,073
Line three: she keeps the rest on success.

611
00:43:17,073 --> 00:43:21,689
R b equals R minus R l: one hundred and sixty minus one hundred, sixty.

612
00:43:21,689 --> 00:43:23,535
Line four: check the incentive constraint.

613
00:43:23,535 --> 00:43:27,535
She needs R b at least B over delta p, which is fifty.

614
00:43:27,535 --> 00:43:28,458
She has sixty.

615
00:43:28,458 --> 00:43:30,919
Sixty is at least fifty, so she behaves.

616
00:43:30,919 --> 00:43:32,150
The contract is self-enforcing.

617
00:43:32,150 --> 00:43:33,381
Line five: her payoff.

618
00:43:33,381 --> 00:43:37,073
On success, with probability zero point eight, she gets sixty: expected forty-eight.

619
00:43:37,073 --> 00:43:39,227
Subtract the twenty she put in: twenty-eight.

620
00:43:39,227 --> 00:43:40,765
Exactly the net present value.

621
00:43:40,765 --> 00:43:44,765
The lenders broke even, so all the value the project creates is hers.

622
00:43:44,765 --> 00:43:46,304
Line six: the interest rate.

623
00:43:46,304 --> 00:43:50,304
Tirole writes the lenders' success payment as one plus iota, times the loan.

624
00:43:50,304 --> 00:43:59,535
One hundred equals one plus iota, times eighty, so one plus iota is one point two five: a nominal rate of twenty-five per cent, with the riskless rate at zero.

625
00:43:59,535 --> 00:44:02,612
Line seven: and that is exactly one over p H.

626
00:44:02,612 --> 00:44:05,688
One over zero point eight is one point two five.

627
00:44:05,688 --> 00:44:12,458
The twenty-five per cent is pure default premium — compensation for the twenty per cent chance of failure — and nothing else.

628
00:44:12,458 --> 00:44:19,227
Now notice what does not appear: the sister pays no penalty for being watched, and no extra interest for the moral hazard.

629
00:44:19,227 --> 00:44:23,227
Once she is above the threshold, moral hazard costs her nothing at all.

630
00:44:23,227 --> 00:44:25,073
Below it, it costs her everything.

631
00:44:25,073 --> 00:44:29,381
The cost of the agency problem is not a smooth premium on the loan.

632
00:44:29,381 --> 00:44:30,611
It is a cliff.

633
00:44:40,641 --> 00:44:42,487
The cliff is at A bar.

634
00:44:42,487 --> 00:44:44,333
So the question "who gets funded?"

635
00:44:44,333 --> 00:44:45,872
becomes "what moves A bar?"

636
00:44:45,872 --> 00:44:49,872
We can answer it line by line, by differentiating equation three point three.

637
00:44:49,872 --> 00:44:55,102
Recall it: A bar equals p H B over delta p, minus, p H R minus I.

638
00:44:55,102 --> 00:44:56,641
Line one: the private benefit.

639
00:44:56,641 --> 00:45:01,256
The derivative of A bar with respect to B is p H over delta p.

640
00:45:01,256 --> 00:45:04,641
With our numbers, zero point eight over zero point four: two.

641
00:45:04,641 --> 00:45:08,641
Every extra thousand reais of private benefit raises the threshold by two thousand.

642
00:45:08,641 --> 00:45:11,718
A firm where insiders can divert more needs richer owners.

643
00:45:11,718 --> 00:45:13,256
Line two: the success income.

644
00:45:13,256 --> 00:45:17,564
The derivative with respect to R is minus p H: minus zero point eight.

645
00:45:17,564 --> 00:45:24,025
A more valuable project lowers the threshold — but by less than one for one, because only the success state pays.

646
00:45:24,025 --> 00:45:25,256
Line three: the investment.

647
00:45:25,256 --> 00:45:28,025
The derivative with respect to I is plus one.

648
00:45:28,025 --> 00:45:32,025
Every extra real of investment must be found, real for real, by somebody.

649
00:45:32,025 --> 00:45:33,256
Line four: the probabilities.

650
00:45:33,256 --> 00:45:42,795
Divide the rent by p H, and it depends on delta p over p H — what Tirole calls the likelihood ratio: how much more likely success is when she behaves.

651
00:45:42,795 --> 00:45:48,948
The larger that ratio, the more informative success is about her behaviour, the smaller the rent, the lower the threshold.

652
00:45:48,948 --> 00:45:50,795
So, read across the four lines.

653
00:45:50,795 --> 00:46:00,333
Funding is easier when insiders have more of their own money, when they can divert less, when the project is more valuable or cheaper, and when outcomes reveal more about behaviour.

654
00:46:00,333 --> 00:46:03,410
Each of those is the subject of a later class.

655
00:46:03,410 --> 00:46:09,564
Tirole's section on the determinants of borrowing capacity — Class seven — is this list, one item at a time.

656
00:46:17,106 --> 00:46:20,491
Now draw what the founder gets, against how much she has.

657
00:46:20,491 --> 00:46:22,645
On the horizontal axis, her assets A.

658
00:46:22,645 --> 00:46:28,799
On the vertical axis, her utility U b — what she ends up with, net of what she put in.

659
00:46:28,799 --> 00:46:32,799
Below the threshold A bar, she is not funded, and she gets zero.

660
00:46:32,799 --> 00:46:39,260
At the threshold and above, she is funded, competitive lenders break even, and she collects the whole net present value: twenty-eight.

661
00:46:39,260 --> 00:46:40,799
Tirole's equation three point six.

662
00:46:40,799 --> 00:46:46,029
So the curve is flat at zero, and then jumps to twenty-eight at A bar equals twelve.

663
00:46:46,029 --> 00:46:48,799
Here is our founder: a red dot at ten.

664
00:46:48,799 --> 00:46:51,260
Two units to the left of the jump.

665
00:46:51,260 --> 00:46:55,260
Her project is worth exactly as much as it would be at twelve.

666
00:46:55,260 --> 00:46:56,799
She loses all of it.

667
00:46:56,799 --> 00:46:58,953
And she is not the only loser.

668
00:46:58,953 --> 00:47:07,260
The twenty-eight thousand reais was value for the economy, not just for her: the output that would have been produced, the jobs it would have paid for.

669
00:47:07,260 --> 00:47:10,029
When the project is not funded, nobody gets it.

670
00:47:10,029 --> 00:47:17,106
The investors are no worse off — they keep their ninety — but the economy is twenty-eight poorer than it could have been.

671
00:47:17,106 --> 00:47:24,799
That is what economists mean by credit rationing being inefficient: a transaction that would make someone better off and nobody worse off does not happen.

672
00:47:24,799 --> 00:47:27,260
Now play the comparative statics on the picture.

673
00:47:27,260 --> 00:47:31,876
Give her more of her own wealth and the dot moves right, across the jump.

674
00:47:31,876 --> 00:47:35,568
Raise B by one and the jump itself moves right by two.

675
00:47:35,568 --> 00:47:38,953
Make success more informative about behaviour and the jump moves left.

676
00:47:38,953 --> 00:47:45,414
Every policy that helps founders like her works through one of those two motions: move the dot, or move the cliff.

677
00:47:45,414 --> 00:47:47,260
And here Brazil comes back in.

678
00:47:47,260 --> 00:47:52,491
One natural reading of B is how much an insider can take from investors without being stopped.

679
00:47:52,491 --> 00:47:58,953
Tirole notes that countries with strong investor protection limit managers' ability to squander investor money, and so have lower agency costs.

680
00:47:58,953 --> 00:48:06,030
Courts, disclosure rules, a listing segment that gives minorities a vote, a cross-listing under a stricter regulator — each of these lowers B.

681
00:48:06,030 --> 00:48:10,953
A lower B moves the jump to the left, and firms like our founder's get funded.

682
00:48:10,953 --> 00:48:15,568
That is the precise sense in which institutions change how much finance an economy gets.

683
00:48:25,573 --> 00:48:28,957
One last move, and it closes the loop with the opening.

684
00:48:28,957 --> 00:48:32,957
Take the private benefit B and shrink it, all the way to zero.

685
00:48:32,957 --> 00:48:33,881
Watch the threshold.

686
00:48:33,881 --> 00:48:37,265
A bar equals the agency rent minus the net present value.

687
00:48:37,265 --> 00:48:42,496
As B goes to zero, the agency rent, p H B over delta p, goes to zero.

688
00:48:42,496 --> 00:48:45,573
A bar becomes minus the net present value: minus twenty-eight.

689
00:48:45,573 --> 00:48:48,342
The jump has slid to the left of zero.

690
00:48:48,342 --> 00:48:51,419
Every founder — even one with nothing — is funded.

691
00:48:51,419 --> 00:48:52,957
Our red dot turns green.

692
00:48:52,957 --> 00:49:04,342
With B equal to zero, there is nothing to gain from misbehaving, so behaviour does not need to be bought, so all the expected income is pledgeable, so any project with positive net present value finds money.

693
00:49:04,342 --> 00:49:07,419
The split no longer affects the size of the pie.

694
00:49:07,419 --> 00:49:10,496
That is Modigliani and Miller, recovered as a special case.

695
00:49:10,496 --> 00:49:14,496
There is a subtler echo of Modigliani and Miller inside the model, too.

696
00:49:14,496 --> 00:49:27,727
With only two outcomes, success and failure, it does not matter whether we call the lenders' claim debt or equity: a claim to one hundred and ten on success and zero on failure is both a loan and a share of the income.

697
00:49:27,727 --> 00:49:31,419
Tirole makes exactly this point in section three point two point three.

698
00:49:31,419 --> 00:49:37,265
The model does not tell us which security to issue — only how much can be promised in total.

699
00:49:37,265 --> 00:49:41,573
Choosing between securities becomes interesting later, when outsiders have their own decisions to make.

700
00:49:41,573 --> 00:49:48,342
So the two worlds of this video differ by exactly one thing: whether the insider's behaviour can be written into the contract.

701
00:49:48,342 --> 00:49:50,188
When it can, financing is irrelevant.

702
00:49:50,188 --> 00:49:52,650
When it cannot, financing is the whole problem.

703
00:50:05,173 --> 00:50:07,019
Here is the picture to keep.

704
00:50:07,019 --> 00:50:10,096
The step: zero, then a jump to twenty-eight at twelve.

705
00:50:10,096 --> 00:50:11,634
Our founder, red, at ten.

706
00:50:11,634 --> 00:50:19,019
Above it, her pie: a blue slice of fifty that she must keep, and a teal slice — all that investors can be promised.

707
00:50:19,019 --> 00:50:23,634
Modigliani and Miller is the world where the step sits to the left of zero.

708
00:50:23,634 --> 00:50:26,403
Corporate finance is the world where it does not.

709
00:50:26,403 --> 00:50:29,480
The rest of the course moves pieces of this picture.

710
00:50:29,480 --> 00:50:32,250
Valuation, in Classes two and three, prices the pie.

711
00:50:32,250 --> 00:50:36,250
Classes four and five finish Modigliani and Miller, with taxes and default costs.

712
00:50:36,250 --> 00:50:41,480
Classes six to eight extend the model you just derived — borrowing capacity, debt overhang, control rights.

713
00:50:41,480 --> 00:50:44,865
Liquidity asks what happens when the pie needs more money midway.

714
00:50:44,865 --> 00:50:49,788
Asymmetric information asks what happens when the lender does not know how big the pie is.

715
00:50:49,788 --> 00:50:52,250
Activism and takeovers ask who watches the insider.

716
00:50:52,250 --> 00:50:58,096
And the last classes ask what happens when every founder in an economy is near the jump at once.

717
00:50:58,096 --> 00:51:07,634
For practice, Tirole's exercises three point one to three point three, on page one hundred and forty-four, use exactly this model, and their answers are in the back of the book.

718
00:51:07,634 --> 00:51:14,096
Before you open them, try one thing yourself: redo our founder's numbers with a private benefit of fifteen instead of twenty.

719
00:51:14,096 --> 00:51:19,634
Compute the minimum stake, the pledgeable income, and A bar — and decide whether she gets the money.

720
00:51:19,634 --> 00:51:20,558
Ten against twelve.

721
00:51:20,558 --> 00:51:23,327
Two thousand reais of something that is not money.

722
00:51:23,327 --> 00:51:24,558
That is corporate finance.
